        {
          "kind": "box",
          "rect": [
            531,
            326,
            354,
            182
          ],
          "color": "#C00000"
        }
      ],
      "speech": "Why not warp the noise like an image? Top left, a roller coaster zooming forward. Top right, nearest-neighbor copies the same noise pixel into streaks. Bottom left, bilinear averages neighbors: average four unit-variance values and variance drops as low as one quarter, so it goes gray, and neighbors share inputs, so it goes blurry. Both have strong spatial correlation, that's the I; ours has none. Other Gaussian warps get that too; ours has the lowest warping error at about InfRes speed. And the model notices: in DeepFloyd super-resolution, bilinear noise doubles LPIPS, 0.29 to 0.60."
    },
    {
      "title": "Noise as particles",
      "elements": [
        {
          "kind": "image",
          "path": "/Users/rburgert/Documents/ThesisProposal/presentation/agents/.scratchpad/explain_gwtf/algo_graph_fixed.png",
          "rect": [
            246,
            84,
            468,
            311
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "X_i = q/d + (Z_i − S/d)/√d",
          "rect": [
            180,
            400,
            600,
            32
          ],
          "size": 24,
          "color": "#000000"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "d = edges out of q,  Z_i = fresh noise,  S = Σ Z_i",
          "rect": [
            130,
            436,
            700,
            28
          ],
          "size": 20,
          "color": "#000000"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Pieces are jointly Gaussian with zero covariance, so independent.",
          "rect": [
            80,
            474,
            800,
            34
          ],
          "size": 24,
          "color": "#C00000"
        }
      ],
      "speech": "So how? Each noise pixel is a particle. Green arrows push particles forward along the flow; three land on q-prime-two. The orange pixels are holes nobody landed on, so blue arrows pull from q-two along the backward flow. Now q-two has three edges, contraction included, so we split it into three pieces with this formula from How I Warped Your Noise: q over d, plus fresh noise with its mean removed, so the pieces still sum to q. The pieces are jointly Gaussian with zero covariance, so they're independent, and each is used exactly once. Each target adds its pieces weighted by alpha, p over d, then rescales back to variance one, so if frame t is i.i.d. Gaussian, so is t plus one. The red p is density. q-prime-two now stands for seven-thirds of a pixel, so later merges weight it that much. And it's linear time, 26 times faster than How I Warped Your Noise."
    },
