          "text": "DL3DV camera: mIoU 0.83 vs 0.71 (MotionClone)",
          "rect": [
            30,
            430,
            900,
            32
          ],
          "size": 20,
          "color": "#000000"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "untuned CogVideoX 0.52 · ours γ=0.9 0.50 · ours γ=0.5 0.74",
          "rect": [
            30,
            468,
            900,
            32
          ],
          "size": 20,
          "color": "#C00000"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "DAVIS mIoU",
          "rect": [
            690,
            206,
            260,
            28
          ],
          "size": 20,
          "color": "#000000"
        }
      ],
      "speech": "Why fine-tune? Untuned CogVideoX roughly follows warped noise, but with visible artifacts. Each frame is still Gaussian, but across time it deliberately isn't, and it's never seen that. So we warp noise in pixel space for four million videos, mean-pool it to the latent times 8 so variance stays one, and train a rank-2048 LoRA, 40 A100-days. No new modules: the LoRA merges back in, so zero extra inference cost. Gamma is how much fresh noise we mix in; it's random during training, so at inference you just pick it. Lower is stricter: on DAVIS, gamma 0.5 gives tracking mIoU 0.74, while 0.9 is nearly fresh noise, so control fades to 0.50, no better than untuned CogVideoX at 0.52. Top row is the input motion, a crude cut-and-paste; bottom row: the cat actually yawns. On VIPSeg objects we beat SG-I2V and MotionClone on FID and mIoU; on DL3DV camera we beat MotionClone."
